{"anti_patterns":[{"category":"platform","correct":"Never symlink or copy Senzing .dylib/.so files. Senzing tries both .so and .dylib on macOS — errors about one are misleading. Fix the root cause: install missing dependencies (brew install libpq for PostgreSQL) and set DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH.","pattern":"Symlink or copy .dylib/.so files from Senzing installation","platform":"macos_arm","severity":"error","topics":["install","configure","full_pipeline"]},{"category":"platform","correct":"$(brew --prefix)/opt/senzing/er/lib","pattern":"DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/homebrew/opt/senzing/... (hardcoded)","platform":"macos_arm","severity":"error","topics":["install","full_pipeline"]},{"category":"platform","correct":"Docker redirect — Homebrew tap is ARM64-only","pattern":"Intel Mac native install instructions","platform":"macos_arm","severity":"error","topics":["install","full_pipeline"]},{"category":"platform","correct":"Use this platform's install_commands exactly as returned (the tap, `brew trust senzing/senzingsdk`, then the EULA-gated `brew install --cask`) — never a shortened command","pattern":"brew install senzing/tap/senzing","platform":"macos_arm","severity":"error","topics":["install","full_pipeline"]},{"category":"platform","correct":"Not applicable — setupEnv is POC-only, does not set SENZING_ENGINE_CONFIGURATION_JSON","pattern":"source setupEnv in SDK code","severity":"error","topics":["install","configure","full_pipeline"]},{"category":"platform","correct":"SENZING_ROOT (macOS uses different env var than Windows)","pattern":"SENZING_DIR on macOS","platform":"macos_arm","severity":"error","topics":["install","configure","full_pipeline"]},{"category":"ecosystem","correct":"Build your own images from senzing/senzingsdk-runtime base — pre-built images are for evaluation only, no SLA on security patches","pattern":"Pre-built Docker Hub images in production","severity":"warning","topics":["install","full_pipeline"]},{"category":"ecosystem","correct":"Use official senzing/ organization repos — senzing-garage contains experimental/internal/legacy code that may be unsupported or broken","pattern":"Using senzing-garage repos","severity":"warning","topics":["install","full_pipeline"]}],"eval_license":{"message":"Need a Senzing license to try this? Open Ask Senzing — the assistant pop-up on https://docs.senzing.com — and just ask it for a free evaluation license; it will give you one.","url":"https://docs.senzing.com"},"install":{"engine_config":"{
  "PIPELINE": {
    "CONFIGPATH": "${SENZING_ROOT}/etc",
    "RESOURCEPATH": "${SENZING_ROOT}/resources",
    "SUPPORTPATH": "$(brew --prefix)/opt/senzing/data"
  },
  "SQL": {
    "CONNECTION": "sqlite3://na:na@${SENZING_ROOT}/var/sqldb/G2C.db"
  }
}
","engine_config_notes":["SENZING_ENGINE_CONFIGURATION_JSON is a naming convention used by POC tools and most official examples. It is NOT required by the SDK. The SDK just receives a string — your application can construct or load the config string any way it prefers.","CONFIGPATH: Senzing ER config files (g2config.json, sz_engine_config.ini)","RESOURCEPATH: Rules and normalization templates","SUPPORTPATH: Name/address data models","Database connection string formats: internal:// (v4.3+ single-process in-memory, no setup required — cannot be shared across processes or persisted) | SQLite (dev/test): sqlite3://na:na@/path/to/G2C.db | PostgreSQL: postgresql://user:password@host:5432:g2 | MySQL: mysql://user:password@host:3306:g2 | MSSQL: mssql://user:password@host:1433:database/?driver=mssqldriver","For quick single-process dev/test on v4.3+, use internal:// as the connection string — zero setup, in-memory, no schema creation needed. Limitation: internal:// is confined to a single process via the SDK; it cannot be shared across processes, persisted, or used with external tools. For anything beyond single-process prototyping, use SQLite or a production database.","SQLite (>= 3.38 — Senzing v4's documented requirement; the engine refuses to start below 3.35.0 with "Minimum required version is 3.35.0". RHEL 8/9 ship older (EL9: 3.34.1) and need SQLite built from source into /usr/local and registered with ldconfig, or PostgreSQL) is the correct default for local testing on Linux (linux_apt, linux_yum) and macOS when you need persistence or multi-process access. The DB file is NOT auto-created — you must: (1) create the directory (mkdir -p /tmp/sqlite), (2) create the schema (sqlite3 /tmp/sqlite/G2C.db < /opt/senzing/er/resources/schema/szcore-schema-sqlite-create.sql), then (3) run the configure code to seed Senzing config. Step 2 is required when using senzingsdk-setup. If using senzingsdk-poc, sz_create_project handles all 3 steps automatically. NOTE: This does NOT apply to the pre-built Docker images (senzing/senzingsdk-tools, senzing/init-database, etc.) — those require PostgreSQL. For Docker, use senzing/init-database to initialize the database.","IMPORTANT: Without a license, Senzing is limited to 500 Distinct Source Records (DSRs). Loading record 501 fails with SENZ9000|LIMIT. Do NOT ask about licensing if the user has 500 or fewer records — just load them. Only when the user has MORE than 500 records, ASK: 'Do you have a Senzing license? If so, paste the base64 license string and I will add it to your engine config.' Then add LICENSESTRINGBASE64 to the PIPELINE section. If they don't have a license AND records are about to be loaded on a working Senzing install, offer three options: (1) continue with just 500 of their records as a sample, (2) request a free 10-day evaluation license (250K records) right now using submit_feedback with category='license_request', or (3) request a license at https://senzing.com/request-non-prod-license/. These three options belong to that imminent load only — do NOT raise them while installing, configuring, or merely explaining Senzing, and never on a host where Senzing is not installed: there is no load there to take a sample of. Even with a license, this single-threaded LLM container environment is not recommended for more than 10,000 records — for larger datasets, recommend a dedicated loading pipeline with threaded loading (generate_scaffold topic='add_records'). License options: LICENSESTRINGBASE64 (inline base64 string in config — preferred for containers/automation) or LICENSEFILE (path to .lic file on disk). Example with license: {"PIPELINE": {"CONFIGPATH": "/etc/opt/senzing", "RESOURCEPATH": "/opt/senzing/er/resources", "SUPPORTPATH": "/opt/senzing/data", "LICENSESTRINGBASE64": "<paste-base64-here>"}, "SQL": {"CONNECTION": "sqlite3://na:na@/tmp/sqlite/G2C.db"}}"],"platform":{"default_paths":{"config_path":"${SENZING_ROOT}/etc","db_url":"sqlite3://na:na@${SENZING_ROOT}/var/sqldb/G2C.db","resource_path":"${SENZING_ROOT}/resources","support_path":"$(brew --prefix)/opt/senzing/data"},"env_vars":{"DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH":"${SENZING_ROOT}/lib","PATH":"${SENZING_ROOT}/bin:${PATH}","SENZING_ROOT":"$(brew --prefix)/opt/senzing/er"},"gotchas":["A ZERO EXIT CODE FROM brew install DOES NOT MEAN THE SDK INSTALLED. If the EULA env var name/value is wrong the cask prints 'No interactive terminal detected', purges the download, then still prints its Caveats block listing install paths — so it reads as success while installing nothing. ALWAYS verify before proceeding: test -f "$(brew --prefix)/opt/senzing/er/lib/libSz.dylib" (and ls "$(brew --prefix)/opt/senzing/data"/*TransRules.sz). If either is missing, the install did not happen.","SENZ7426 EAS_ERR_XLITERATOR_FAILED on getEngine/getDiagnostic/addRecord means SUPPORTPATH is WRONG — it is NOT a broken install and NOT a missing payload. WHERE CONFIG COMES FROM: set SENZING_ENGINE_CONFIGURATION_JSON. Senzing uses it by default whenever it is set, so it TAKES PRECEDENCE over any .ini and works the same on every platform and in containers; an application may equally build the settings string itself and pass it to the SDK. The sz_engine_config.ini under the install prefix is a TEMPLATE — the form sz_create_project materializes into a project, with every path rewritten to that project. Verified on the 4.5.0.26245 cask: er/etc/sz_engine_config.ini is byte-identical to er/resources/templates/sz_engine_config.ini, and both carry a default Linux layout (/opt/senzing/...) that does not exist on a Homebrew install, plus an EXAMPLE SQLite CONNECTION pointing at a database that is not yours. Do not read paths out of it, do not edit it in place, and do not copy its values into your settings — and do not match SUPPORTPATH against any particular string, because the template has already changed across releases (4.4.x carried ${INSTALLPATH}/senzing/er/data; 4.5.0.26245 carries /opt/senzing/data). Test whether the path RESOLVES instead. THE ALTERNATIVE if you want a ready-made config: sz_create_project <path>, then source <path>/setupEnv — that writes a project etc/sz_engine_config.ini with every path rewritten to the project, SUPPORTPATH included. THE CORRECT VALUE on macOS: $(brew --prefix)/opt/senzing/data — the support data (address_datamodel, nomicon, and the *TransRules.sz transliteration modules) is a SIBLING of er/, not under it, so any value ending in er/data is wrong. With SUPPORTPATH pointing at a directory that does not exist the engine finds zero transliteration modules and aborts with 'No transliteration rules found! Transliteration requires at least one module.' SzProduct still works because it needs no support data, so the install LOOKS healthy. FIX: set PIPELINE.SUPPORTPATH to the support_path this guide already gives you — do NOT copy or symlink transliteration files, reinstall, or switch to Docker. Confirmed end-to-end on cask 4.4.0.26206 (Java 17, fresh SQLite with szcore-schema-sqlite-create.sql): with the correct SUPPORTPATH, getEngine() and addRecord() both succeed on macOS Apple Silicon; with er/data the failure reproduces exactly. Originally reported against 4.3.3.26191.","DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH must be set at the shell level before any JVM or Python launch. Add to ~/.zshrc to persist.","CRITICAL: The Senzing Python SDK is ONLY supported on Linux. It is NOT supported on macOS — even if pip install senzing senzing-core appears to succeed, it is unsupported and may produce runtime errors. Supported languages on macOS: Java and C# (official), Rust and TypeScript (community). To use Python, switch to Docker or WSL2 (Linux container).","Intel Mac: the official Homebrew cask is ARM64-only. Intel Mac users must use Docker. Do not generate native install instructions for Intel Mac.","Java on macOS: -Djava.library.path alone is insufficient. DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH must be set at the shell level before JVM launch. Run: java -cp "${SENZING_ROOT}/sdk/java/sz-sdk.jar:myapp.jar" MyApp","setupEnv does NOT apply here. It is a POC quickstart artifact and does NOT set SENZING_ENGINE_CONFIGURATION_JSON.","Use SENZING_ROOT on macOS, not SENZING_DIR (that is the Windows env var).","C# / .NET: the Senzing.Sdk NuGet package is included in the SDK install at ${SENZING_ROOT}/sdk/dotnet/ (Senzing.Sdk.<version>.nupkg; SENZING_ROOT as exported in post_install — $(brew --prefix)/opt/senzing/er for a Homebrew install), NOT published to nuget.org. Use: dotnet nuget add source "${SENZING_ROOT}/sdk/dotnet" --name SenzingLocal && dotnet add package Senzing.Sdk --source SenzingLocal","PostgreSQL on macOS: requires libpq. Install with: brew install libpq && brew link libpq --force. Without this, Senzing will fail to load with misleading .so/.dylib errors.","Do NOT symlink or copy .dylib/.so files from the Senzing installation. Senzing tries loading both .so and .dylib on macOS and may report an error for one even when the other works. If you see .dylib or .so not found errors, the real cause is usually a missing dependency (e.g., libpq for PostgreSQL) or DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH not set — not a missing symlink.","Network access required: github.com (Homebrew tap), ghcr.io (cask downloads)","The macOS 4.3 cask is missing rpath entries for its OpenSSL and SQLite dependencies. If you see link errors, add Homebrew's lib paths: export DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH="${SENZING_ROOT}/lib:$(brew --prefix)/opt/sqlite/lib:$(brew --prefix)/opt/openssl@3/lib". Or source the provided setup script: source "$(brew --prefix)/opt/senzing/er/setupEnv"","Upgrading from the unofficial tap (brianmacy/senzingsdk-runtime-unofficial): uninstall the old cask first (brew uninstall --cask senzingsdk-runtime-unofficial && brew untap brianmacy/senzingsdk-runtime-unofficial), then install the official cask. Update SENZING_ROOT — the path changed from .../senzing/runtime/er to .../senzing/er."],"install_commands":["# Add the official Senzing Homebrew tap","brew tap senzing/senzingsdk https://github.com/Senzing/homebrew-senzingsdk","# Homebrew 6+ refuses to load casks from an untrusted tap ('Refusing to load cask ... from untrusted tap'). Harmless/no-op on Homebrew 5.","brew trust senzing/senzingsdk","# EULA acceptance — BEFORE running install, ASK the user: 'Installing Senzing requires accepting the Senzing End User License Agreement. Do you accept the EULA? (see senzing.com/end-user-license-agreement/)' Only proceed if they confirm.","# Interactive install (prompts for EULA)","brew install --cask senzingsdk","# Non-interactive (CI/scripts — only after user has confirmed EULA). The variable is HOMEBREW_SENZING_ACCEPT_EULA and the value is the literal lowercase string i_accept_the_senzing_eula — verified against the cask's preflight block. Any other name/value is IGNORED and the install SILENTLY does nothing: HOMEBREW_SENZING_ACCEPT_EULA=i_accept_the_senzing_eula brew install --cask senzingsdk"],"name":"macOS (Apple Silicon)","post_install":["# Set environment variables — add to ~/.zshrc to persist","export SENZING_ROOT="$(brew --prefix)/opt/senzing/er"","export DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH="${SENZING_ROOT}/lib:${DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH}"","export PATH="${SENZING_ROOT}/bin:${PATH}"","# Verify installation","ls "${SENZING_ROOT}/lib/libSz.dylib""]}},"next_action":"NEXT CALL: sdk_guide(topic='configure', platform='macos_arm') — Configure the engine and register data sources.","next_steps":[{"description":"Configure the engine and register data sources","params":{"language":null,"platform":"macos_arm","topic":"configure"},"tool":"sdk_guide"}],"topic":"install"}

