","engine_config_notes":["SENZING_ENGINE_CONFIGURATION_JSON is a naming convention used by POC tools and most official examples. It is NOT required by the SDK. The SDK just receives a string — your application can construct or load the config string any way it prefers.","CONFIGPATH: Senzing ER config files (g2config.json, sz_engine_config.ini)","RESOURCEPATH: Rules and normalization templates","SUPPORTPATH: Name/address data models","Database connection string formats: internal:// (v4.3+ single-process in-memory, no setup required — cannot be shared across processes or persisted) | SQLite (dev/test): sqlite3://na:na@/path/to/G2C.db | PostgreSQL: postgresql://user:password@host:5432:g2 | MySQL: mysql://user:password@host:3306:g2 | MSSQL: mssql://user:password@host:1433:database/?driver=mssqldriver","For quick single-process dev/test on v4.3+, use internal:// as the connection string — zero setup, in-memory, no schema creation needed. Limitation: internal:// is confined to a single process via the SDK; it cannot be shared across processes, persisted, or used with external tools. For anything beyond single-process prototyping, use SQLite or a production database.","SQLite (>= 3.38 — Senzing v4's documented requirement; the engine refuses to start below 3.35.0 with "Minimum required version is 3.35.0". RHEL 8/9 ship older (EL9: 3.34.1) and need SQLite built from source into /usr/local and registered with ldconfig, or PostgreSQL) is the correct default for local testing on Linux (linux_apt, linux_yum) and macOS when you need persistence or multi-process access. The DB file is NOT auto-created — you must: (1) create the directory (mkdir -p /tmp/sqlite), (2) create the schema (sqlite3 /tmp/sqlite/G2C.db < /opt/senzing/er/resources/schema/szcore-schema-sqlite-create.sql), then (3) run the configure code to seed Senzing config. Step 2 is required when using senzingsdk-setup. If using senzingsdk-poc, sz_create_project handles all 3 steps automatically. NOTE: This does NOT apply to the pre-built Docker images (senzing/senzingsdk-tools, senzing/init-database, etc.) — those require PostgreSQL. For Docker, use senzing/init-database to initialize the database.","IMPORTANT: Without a license, Senzing is limited to 500 Distinct Source Records (DSRs). Loading record 501 fails with SENZ9000|LIMIT. Do NOT ask about licensing if the user has 500 or fewer records — just load them. Only when the user has MORE than 500 records, ASK: 'Do you have a Senzing license? If so, paste the base64 license string and I will add it to your engine config.' Then add LICENSESTRINGBASE64 to the PIPELINE section. If they don't have a license AND records are about to be loaded on a working Senzing install, offer three options: (1) continue with just 500 of their records as a sample, (2) request a free 10-day evaluation license (250K records) right now using submit_feedback with category='license_request', or (3) request a license at https://senzing.com/request-non-prod-license/. These three options belong to that imminent load only — do NOT raise them while installing, configuring, or merely explaining Senzing, and never on a host where Senzing is not installed: there is no load there to take a sample of. Even with a license, this single-threaded LLM container environment is not recommended for more than 10,000 records — for larger datasets, recommend a dedicated loading pipeline with threaded loading (generate_scaffold topic='add_records'). License options: LICENSESTRINGBASE64 (inline base64 string in config — preferred for containers/automation) or LICENSEFILE (path to .lic file on disk). Example with license: {"PIPELINE": {"CONFIGPATH": "/etc/opt/senzing", "RESOURCEPATH": "/opt/senzing/er/resources", "SUPPORTPATH": "/opt/senzing/data", "LICENSESTRINGBASE64": "<paste-base64-here>"}, "SQL": {"CONNECTION": "sqlite3://na:na@/tmp/sqlite/G2C.db"}}"],"platform":{"default_paths":{"config_path":"%SENZING_DIR%\etc","db_url":"sqlite3://na:na@%SENZING_DIR%\var\sqldb\G2C.db","resource_path":"%SENZING_DIR%\resources","support_path":"%SENZING_DIR%\..\data"},"env_vars":{"PATH":"er\lib added automatically by Scoop","SENZING_DIR":"Set automatically by Scoop to <scoop-app-dir>\er — NOTE this points at the `er` subdirectory, NOT the Senzing root. The support data is the root's SIBLING `data` directory, so SUPPORTPATH must NOT be built by appending to %SENZING_DIR%."},"gotchas":["SUPPORTPATH IS NOT UNDER %SENZING_DIR%. Scoop sets SENZING_DIR=<scoop-app-dir>\er, but the support data (address_datamodel, *TransRules.sz transliteration modules, szBuildVersion.json) installs to <scoop-app-dir>\data — a SIBLING of er, not a child. Use %SENZING_DIR%\..\data, set as PIPELINE.SUPPORTPATH in SENZING_ENGINE_CONFIGURATION_JSON (which takes precedence whenever it is set) or in the settings string your application passes to the SDK — not by editing the sz_engine_config.ini under the install prefix. Building SUPPORTPATH as %SENZING_DIR%\data yields <dir>\er\data, which does not exist, and every SzEngine/SzDiagnostic call then fails with SENZ7426 EAS_ERR_XLITERATOR_FAILED ('No transliteration rules found! Transliteration requires at least one module') while SzProduct keeps working — so the install looks healthy. CONFIGPATH and RESOURCEPATH ARE under %SENZING_DIR% (er\etc, er\resources); SUPPORTPATH is the sole exception. Verified against the MSI Directory table for 4.3.3.26191 (ProgramFiles64Folder\Senzing\{data,er}).","CRITICAL: The Senzing Python SDK is ONLY supported on Linux. It is NOT supported on Windows — even if pip install appears to succeed, it is unsupported and may produce runtime errors. Supported languages on Windows: Java and C# (official), Rust and TypeScript (community). To use Python, switch to Docker or WSL2 (Linux container).","Java on Windows: no -Djava.library.path needed — er\lib is already on PATH after Scoop install. Run: $env:CLASSPATH = "$env:SENZING_DIR\sdk\java\sz-sdk.jar;$env:CLASSPATH" then java -cp $env:CLASSPATH MyApp","C# / .NET: the Senzing.Sdk NuGet package is included in the SDK install (sdk\dotnet\), NOT published to nuget.org. Use: dotnet nuget add source "$env:SENZING_DIR\sdk\dotnet" --name SenzingLocal; dotnet add package Senzing.Sdk --source SenzingLocal","Verify installation: echo $env:SENZING_DIR; Test-Path "$env:SENZING_DIR\lib\Sz.dll"","Network access required: github.com (Scoop bucket and installer)"],"install_commands":["# Install Scoop if needed","Set-ExecutionPolicy -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned -Scope CurrentUser","Invoke-RestMethod -Uri https://get.scoop.sh | Invoke-Expression","# Add the official Senzing Scoop bucket and install","scoop bucket add senzingsdk https://github.com/Senzing/scoop-senzingsdk","# EULA acceptance — BEFORE running install, ASK the user: 'Installing Senzing requires accepting the Senzing End User License Agreement. Do you accept the EULA? (see senzing.com/end-user-license-agreement/)' Only proceed if they confirm.","scoop install senzingsdk/senzingsdk","# Non-interactive (CI/scripts — only after user has confirmed EULA). The variable is SENZING_ACCEPT_EULA and the value is the literal I_ACCEPT_THE_SENZING_EULA — verified against the Scoop manifest pre_install block. Any other name/value is IGNORED: $env:SENZING_ACCEPT_EULA = "I_ACCEPT_THE_SENZING_EULA" (before install). Alternative: scoop config SENZING_ACCEPT_EULA I_ACCEPT_THE_SENZING_EULA"],"name":"Windows","post_install":["# Verify installation","echo $env:SENZING_DIR","Test-Path "$env:SENZING_DIR\lib\Sz.dll""]}},"next_action":"NEXT CALL: sdk_guide(topic='configure', platform='windows') — Configure the engine and register data sources.","next_steps":[{"description":"Configure the engine and register data sources","params":{"language":null,"platform":"windows","topic":"configure"},"tool":"sdk_guide"}],"topic":"install"}

