","engine_config_notes":["SENZING_ENGINE_CONFIGURATION_JSON is a naming convention used by POC tools and most official examples. It is NOT required by the SDK. The SDK just receives a string — your application can construct or load the config string any way it prefers.","CONFIGPATH: Senzing ER config files (g2config.json, sz_engine_config.ini)","RESOURCEPATH: Rules and normalization templates","SUPPORTPATH: Name/address data models","Database connection string formats: internal:// (v4.3+ single-process in-memory, no setup required — cannot be shared across processes or persisted) | SQLite (dev/test): sqlite3://na:na@/path/to/G2C.db | PostgreSQL: postgresql://user:password@host:5432:g2 | MySQL: mysql://user:password@host:3306:g2 | MSSQL: mssql://user:password@host:1433:database/?driver=mssqldriver","For quick single-process dev/test on v4.3+, use internal:// as the connection string — zero setup, in-memory, no schema creation needed. Limitation: internal:// is confined to a single process via the SDK; it cannot be shared across processes, persisted, or used with external tools. For anything beyond single-process prototyping, use SQLite or a production database.","SQLite (>= 3.38 — Senzing v4's documented requirement; the engine refuses to start below 3.35.0 with "Minimum required version is 3.35.0". RHEL 8/9 ship older (EL9: 3.34.1) and need SQLite built from source into /usr/local and registered with ldconfig, or PostgreSQL) is the correct default for local testing on Linux (linux_apt, linux_yum) and macOS when you need persistence or multi-process access. The DB file is NOT auto-created — you must: (1) create the directory (mkdir -p /tmp/sqlite), (2) create the schema (sqlite3 /tmp/sqlite/G2C.db < /opt/senzing/er/resources/schema/szcore-schema-sqlite-create.sql), then (3) run the configure code to seed Senzing config. Step 2 is required when using senzingsdk-setup. If using senzingsdk-poc, sz_create_project handles all 3 steps automatically. NOTE: This does NOT apply to the pre-built Docker images (senzing/senzingsdk-tools, senzing/init-database, etc.) — those require PostgreSQL. For Docker, use senzing/init-database to initialize the database.","IMPORTANT: Without a license, Senzing is limited to 500 Distinct Source Records (DSRs). Loading record 501 fails with SENZ9000|LIMIT. Do NOT ask about licensing if the user has 500 or fewer records — just load them. Only when the user has MORE than 500 records, ASK: 'Do you have a Senzing license? If so, paste the base64 license string and I will add it to your engine config.' Then add LICENSESTRINGBASE64 to the PIPELINE section. If they don't have a license AND records are about to be loaded on a working Senzing install, offer three options: (1) continue with just 500 of their records as a sample, (2) request a free 10-day evaluation license (250K records) right now using submit_feedback with category='license_request', or (3) request a license at https://senzing.com/request-non-prod-license/. These three options belong to that imminent load only — do NOT raise them while installing, configuring, or merely explaining Senzing, and never on a host where Senzing is not installed: there is no load there to take a sample of. Even with a license, this single-threaded LLM container environment is not recommended for more than 10,000 records — for larger datasets, recommend a dedicated loading pipeline with threaded loading (generate_scaffold topic='add_records'). License options: LICENSESTRINGBASE64 (inline base64 string in config — preferred for containers/automation) or LICENSEFILE (path to .lic file on disk). Example with license: {"PIPELINE": {"CONFIGPATH": "/etc/opt/senzing", "RESOURCEPATH": "/opt/senzing/er/resources", "SUPPORTPATH": "/opt/senzing/data", "LICENSESTRINGBASE64": "<paste-base64-here>"}, "SQL": {"CONNECTION": "sqlite3://na:na@/tmp/sqlite/G2C.db"}}"],"platform":{"default_paths":{"config_path":"/etc/opt/senzing","db_url":"sqlite3://na:na@/tmp/sqlite/G2C.db","resource_path":"/opt/senzing/er/resources","support_path":"/opt/senzing/data"},"env_vars":{"LD_LIBRARY_PATH":"/opt/senzing/er/lib (only needed if native lib not found automatically)","PYTHONPATH":"/opt/senzing/er/sdk/python (required for Python SDK — the senzing and senzing-core packages ship with senzingsdk-runtime here)"},"gotchas":["LD_LIBRARY_PATH is only needed if the native lib is not found automatically (e.g., custom install location): export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/senzing/er/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH","setupEnv does NOT apply here. It is a POC quickstart artifact generated by sz_create_project for CLI tools only — it does NOT set SENZING_ENGINE_CONFIGURATION_JSON. Never reference it in SDK code paths.","Network access required: senzing-production-apt.s3.amazonaws.com (apt repository)","Alternative for firewalled environments: when this server carries the Senzing SDK packages for this platform, the install response's `direct_download` lists them with the exact filenames, a fetch instruction per package (`url`) and install commands","The senzingrepo package detects your OS and configures the correct apt source (libssl3 for Ubuntu 24.04+/Debian 13+, libssl1.1 for older). Always use senzingrepo — do NOT manually add the legacy dists/stable repo URL, which only serves libssl1.1 builds.","Python SDK: The senzing and senzing-core packages are included in senzingsdk-runtime at /opt/senzing/er/sdk/python. Do NOT pip install them — instead set PYTHONPATH=/opt/senzing/er/sdk/python:$PYTHONPATH and LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/opt/senzing/er/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH","Evaluation mode: Without a license, Senzing limits ingestion to 500 records (error SENZ9000 at record 501). Only if the user has more than 500 records, ask for their base64 license string and add LICENSESTRINGBASE64 to the PIPELINE section of the engine config. If no license is available AND records are about to be loaded on a working Senzing install, offer three options: (1) continue with just 500 of their records as a sample, (2) request a free 10-day evaluation license (250K records) right now using submit_feedback with category='license_request', or (3) request a license at https://senzing.com/request-non-prod-license/. These three options belong to that imminent load only — do NOT raise them while installing, configuring, or merely explaining Senzing, and never on a host where Senzing is not installed: there is no load there to take a sample of. Even with a license, this single-threaded environment is not recommended for more than 10,000 records — for larger datasets, guide them to a dedicated loading pipeline.","No sudo available (containers, CI, restricted environments): First get user confirmation of the Senzing EULA (see senzing.com/end-user-license-agreement/), then set SENZING_ACCEPT_EULA=I_ACCEPT_THE_SENZING_EULA and extract packages with dpkg-deb instead of apt install. Example: dpkg-deb -x senzingsdk-runtime_*.deb /opt/senzing && dpkg-deb -x senzingsdk-setup_*.deb /opt/senzing — then adjust CONFIGPATH, RESOURCEPATH, SUPPORTPATH, PYTHONPATH, and LD_LIBRARY_PATH to point into your extraction directory.","C# / .NET: the Senzing.Sdk NuGet package is included in the SDK install at /opt/senzing/er/sdk/dotnet/ (senzingsdk-runtime installs Senzing.Sdk.<version>.nupkg there), NOT published to nuget.org. Use: dotnet nuget add source /opt/senzing/er/sdk/dotnet --name SenzingLocal && dotnet add package Senzing.Sdk --source SenzingLocal","Direct-download .deb packages: senzingsdk-runtime, senzingsdk-setup, and senzingsdk-tools are available as direct downloads. Note: senzingsdk-setup has an apt dependency on senzingsdk-tools, but when using dpkg-deb -x extraction you only need senzingsdk-runtime and senzingsdk-setup."],"install_commands":["# Prerequisites — minimal images (debian:bookworm-slim, ubuntu:24.04) ship neither wget, ca-certificates nor sqlite3. In a container you are root: run every command below WITHOUT sudo.","sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y wget ca-certificates sqlite3","# Install the senzingrepo package to configure the correct apt source for your OS","wget https://senzing-production-apt.s3.amazonaws.com/senzingrepo_2.0.2-1_all.deb","sudo apt install ./senzingrepo_2.0.2-1_all.deb","sudo apt update","# EULA acceptance — BEFORE running this command, ASK the user: 'Installing Senzing requires accepting the Senzing End User License Agreement. Do you accept the EULA? (see senzing.com/end-user-license-agreement/)' Only proceed if they confirm.","export SENZING_ACCEPT_EULA=I_ACCEPT_THE_SENZING_EULA","# Minimal SDK install (runtime + setup) — sufficient for SDK-only workflows","sudo apt install -y senzingsdk-runtime senzingsdk-setup","# If you cannot run apt install (containers, CI, no sudo), extract the packages directly instead:","#   dpkg-deb -x senzingsdk-runtime_*.deb /opt/senzing && dpkg-deb -x senzingsdk-setup_*.deb /opt/senzing","#   Then set CONFIGPATH, RESOURCEPATH, SUPPORTPATH, PYTHONPATH, and LD_LIBRARY_PATH to point into your extraction directory."],"name":"Linux (Debian/Ubuntu)","post_install":["# Verify installation","ls /opt/senzing/er/lib/libSz.so","# Create SQLite database directory (the DB file is NOT auto-created)","mkdir -p /tmp/sqlite","# Create the SQLite database schema (required for senzingsdk-setup; senzingsdk-poc's sz_create_project does this automatically)","sqlite3 /tmp/sqlite/G2C.db < /opt/senzing/er/resources/schema/szcore-schema-sqlite-create.sql"],"prerequisites":["wget and ca-certificates (to download the senzingrepo .deb) and the sqlite3 CLI (post_install uses it to create the SQLite schema). Minimal images such as debian:bookworm-slim ship NONE of these — the first install command installs them.","Inside a container you are already root and sudo is NOT installed: run every command below WITHOUT sudo."]}},"next_action":"NEXT CALL: sdk_guide(topic='configure', platform='linux_apt') — Configure the engine and register data sources.","next_steps":[{"description":"Configure the engine and register data sources","params