{"status":"ok","step":3,"step_name":"map_fields","message":"Entity plan validated. Map source fields to Senzing attributes.","instructions":"ADVANCE FORMAT: {"schema_mappings": [{"schema_name": "<name>", "field_mappings": [{"source_field": "<field>", "disposition": "feature", "feature": "<FAMILY>", "attribute": "<ATTR>"}, {"disposition": "derived", "derived_as": "<RECORD_ID|DATA_SOURCE|RECORD_TYPE> — REQUIRED for every derived entry", "source": "<field; for RECORD_ID, and for RECORD_TYPE derived from a type field>", "value": "<constant, for DATA_SOURCE/RECORD_TYPE>", "justification": "<REQUIRED for a constant RECORD_TYPE not wired to a source field>"}]}]}
LARGE MAPPING: send the mapping inline in `payload` as a JSON OBJECT, never a JSON-encoded string.
RULE: a "derived" field_mapping MUST include a "derived_as" key (one of DATA_SOURCE, RECORD_ID, RECORD_TYPE, REL_ANCHOR, REL_POINTER). A "derived" entry without "derived_as" is rejected by the server.

Map ALL fields from every schema to Senzing JSON format. The entity plan (masters, children, relationships) is already validated.

USE THE INLINE REFERENCE: the SENZING MAPPING REFERENCE delivered at step 2 (feature catalog, identifier-classification workflow, and exact ATTRIBUTE KEYS in its section 3) is the authority for feature family names and attribute codes — use it directly. You do NOT need the 73KB entity specification; open it only for a specific edge case the inline reference does not cover. If you no longer have the step-2 reference in context, the feature-family list and key rules below restate the essentials.

READING HINTS:
Consult `/tmp/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-16/schema_hints.md` and the profile report(s) — `/tmp/parity-0.6.1-2026-10-16/profile_report.md` — for how each source file and schema is structured. Use this structural context — nested arrays, discriminated collections, sub-element chains, part-based structures — when mapping fields to Senzing features.

IMPORTANT: Field name ≠ Feature type. If a source field name matches a Senzing attribute (e.g., 'state', 'name', 'address'), verify the data actually serves that purpose per the spec. A field called 'state' might be ADDR_STATE or might be a status field — check the samples.

IMPORTANT: Use Senzing FEATURE FAMILY names, not common names. The feature families are: NAME, ADDRESS, PHONE, EMAIL, SSN, PASSPORT, DRLIC (not DRIVERS_LICENSE), NATIONAL_ID, TAX_ID, OTHER_ID, TRUSTED_ID, ACCOUNT, DUNS_NUMBER, NPI_NUMBER, LEI_NUMBER, DOB, DOD, GENDER, NATIONALITY, CITIZENSHIP, POB, REGISTRATION_DATE, REGISTRATION_COUNTRY, EMPLOYER, GROUP_ASSOCIATION, GROUP_ASSN_ID, REL_ANCHOR, REL_POINTER, WEBSITE, RECORD_TYPE, and social media features (LINKEDIN, FACEBOOK, TWITTER, etc.). The identifier crosswalk maps common ID type tokens to the correct feature family.

DISPOSITIONS:
- "feature": source field maps to a Senzing feature attribute
- "payload": source field kept as root-level metadata (not used for resolution)
- "ignore": source field excluded from output
- "derived": computed attribute not from a source field (constants, hashes, relationships). These include DATA_SOURCE, RECORD_ID, RECORD_TYPE, REL_ANCHOR, and REL_POINTER entries that the mapper must generate. Every entity MUST have derived entries for DATA_SOURCE and RECORD_ID at minimum.
- "extract": source field contains embedded data requiring parsing to produce multiple features. Use when a single field (e.g., OFAC SDN REMARKS) contains DOBs, passports, SSNs, names, nationality, etc. in semi-structured text. Specify expected_features (list of feature families to extract) and optional extraction_notes describing the parsing needed. The mapper code must parse this field and emit the extracted features. Example: {"source_field": "REMARKS", "disposition": "extract", "expected_features": ["DOB", "PASSPORT", "SSN", "NAME", "NATIONALITY", "CITIZENSHIP", "GENDER"], "extraction_notes": "Parse semi-structured text with regex for known patterns like DOB dd Mon yyyy, Passport No. XXX, etc."}

RECORD-LEVEL METADATA vs FEATURES:
RECORD_ID, DATA_SOURCE, and RECORD_TYPE are NOT Senzing features — they are record-level metadata fields that sit at the JSON root. There is no feature family called "RECORD_KEY" or "RECORD_ID". Do NOT map a source field that provides the record identifier as disposition "feature". Instead:
- The source field that provides the RECORD_ID value gets disposition "ignore" (its raw value is not loaded as a feature).
- A separate "derived" entry with derived_as "RECORD_ID" references that source field, telling the mapper code to use its value as the RECORD_ID root attribute.
- DATA_SOURCE is always a "derived" constant (the data source name assigned in Step 2).
- RECORD_TYPE must be PROVENANCED — never a silent constant. If the source has a field that discriminates entity type (e.g. a "Type" column with Individual/Entity values), wire RECORD_TYPE to it: use a type_discriminator (when the type varies by record), or a "derived" RECORD_TYPE that names that field in "source". ONLY if the source is genuinely a single type, use a "derived" constant ("value": "PERSON" or "ORGANIZATION") WITH a non-empty "justification". A bare constant RECORD_TYPE (no "source", no "justification") is REJECTED — this stops a mapper from silently typing every record the same (e.g. all PERSON) when the source actually mixes persons and organizations.

Example — source field "ent_num" provides the record ID:
  {"source_field": "ent_num", "disposition": "ignore", "reason": "Used as RECORD_ID (record-level metadata, not a feature)", "confidence": 1.0},
  {"disposition": "derived", "derived_as": "RECORD_ID", "source": "ent_num", "ref": "Root attribute: from record_id_source"},
  {"disposition": "derived", "derived_as": "DATA_SOURCE", "value": "CUSTOMERS", "ref": "Root attribute: constant per entity"},
  {"disposition": "derived", "derived_as": "RECORD_TYPE", "value": "PERSON", "justification": "source is a customer list of individuals only — no organization records", "ref": "Spec: RECORD_TYPE (constant + justification)"}
(If a field discriminated type instead, you would write: {"disposition": "derived", "derived_as": "RECORD_TYPE", "source": "<type_field>", "ref": "derived from source type field"} — or a type_discriminator.)

