        {"disposition": "derived", "derived_as": "DATA_SOURCE", "value": "<DS>", "ref": "Root attribute: constant per entity"},
        {"disposition": "derived", "derived_as": "RECORD_ID", "source": "<field>", "ref": "Root attribute: from record_id_source"},
        {"disposition": "derived", "derived_as": "RECORD_TYPE", "source": "<type_field>", "ref": "derived from source type field — OR use value+justification for a constant"},
        {"disposition": "derived", "derived_as": "REL_ANCHOR", "domain": "<DOMAIN>", "value": "DOMAIN=<DOMAIN>, KEY=<RECORD_ID>", "ref": "Spec: anchor for disclosed relationships"},
        {"source_field": "<source_field>", "disposition": "extract", "expected_features": ["DOB", "PASSPORT", "SSN", "NAME"], "extraction_notes": "Parse semi-structured text", "confidence": 0.90, "ref": "Field contains multiple embedded features requiring parsing"}
      ],
      "code_mappings": {
        "<discriminator_field>": {
          "value_fields": ["<field_with_value>", "<field_with_country_or_state>"],
          "codes": {
            "<CODE_VALUE>": {
              "feature": "<FEATURE_FAMILY>",
              "attributes": {"<value_field>": "<ATTR>", "<country_field>": "<ATTR>"}
            }
          }
        }
      },
      "type_discriminator": {
        "field": "<source_field>",
        "types": {"<value>": {"record_type": "PERSON|ORGANIZATION", "field_overrides": {}, "constants": {}}},
        "default": {"record_type": null, "field_overrides": {}, "constants": {}}
      }
    }
  ],
  "questions": []
}

CODE MAPPINGS EXAMPLE (id_type discriminates id_number + id_country):
  "code_mappings": {
    "id_type": {
      "value_fields": ["id_number", "id_country"],
      "codes": {
        "DL": {"feature": "DRLIC", "attributes": {"id_number": "DRIVERS_LICENSE_NUMBER", "id_country": "DRIVERS_LICENSE_STATE"}},
        "PP": {"feature": "PASSPORT", "attributes": {"id_number": "PASSPORT_NUMBER", "id_country": "PASSPORT_COUNTRY"}},
        "SS": {"feature": "SSN", "attributes": {"id_number": "SSN_NUMBER"}}
      }
    }
  }
Fields in code_mappings (discriminator + value_fields) do NOT need separate entries in mappings.

CODE FIELD ENUMERATION:
For each code field (discriminator in code_mappings or type_discriminator):
- Check EVERY sample value against the identifier crosswalk
- If a code IS in the crosswalk: map to the correct feature family
- If a code is NOT in the crosswalk: mark as PENDING and add to questions list. Options: map to a suggested feature (with reasoning), use OTHER_ID as catch-all, or ask user
- ALL sample values must be enumerated — do NOT silently skip any
- The server validates that every sample value has a corresponding entry in the codes dict
- For type_discriminator: all sample values must be enumerated in types or covered by default

TYPE DISCRIMINATOR (entity-type conditional logic):
When a source field determines the entity type (PERSON vs ORGANIZATION), use type_discriminator instead of a static derived RECORD_TYPE. This is conditional logic, NOT separate master entities. The discriminator field controls RECORD_TYPE, conditional field mappings, and optional constant injection. See mapping examples Shot Example 2 for the complete pattern.

FORMAT:
  "type_discriminator": {
    "field": "<source_field_that_discriminates>",
    "types": {
      "<value>": {
        "record_type": "PERSON|ORGANIZATION|null",
        "field_overrides": {
          "<source_field>": {"feature": "<FEATURE>", "attribute": "<ATTR>"}
        },
        "constants": {"<ATTR>": "<VALUE>"}
      }
    },
    "default": {
      "record_type": null,
      "field_overrides": {"<source_field>": {"feature": "<FEATURE>", "attribute": "<ATTR>"}},
      "constants": {}
    }
  }

TYPE DISCRIMINATOR EXAMPLE (type field: person vs company):
  "type_discriminator": {
    "field": "type",
    "types": {
      "person": {
        "record_type": "PERSON",
        "field_overrides": {"name": {"feature": "NAME", "attribute": "NAME_FULL"}},
        "constants": {}
      },
      "company": {
        "record_type": "ORGANIZATION",
        "field_overrides": {"name": {"feature": "NAME", "attribute": "NAME_ORG"}},
        "constants": {}
      }
    },
    "default": {
      "record_type": null,
      "field_overrides": {"name": {"feature": "NAME", "attribute": "NAME_FULL"}},
      "constants": {}
    }
  }
Fields in type_discriminator (discriminator field + all override fields) do NOT need separate entries in mappings. The "default" entry handles blank or unrecognized type values.

When type_discriminator is present:
- Do NOT include a derived RECORD_TYPE entry in mappings (the discriminator handles it)
- Fields mapped the SAME way regardless of type stay in regular mappings
- Only fields whose mapping CHANGES based on type go in field_overrides
- constants are extra keys injected INTO an existing feature object of that record (e.g. a PHONE_TYPE the source states, added to the PHONE object a mapped phone field produces) — never a standalone object, and never for a feature no source field maps (the server rejects a lone qualifier such as ADDR_TYPE with no address mapped). Leave constants {} unless the source itself states the value.
- ADDR_TYPE BUSINESS: use that constant ONLY for the organization's PHYSICAL business location, and only when the source identifies the address that way (e.g. a "business/physical/location address" column, or an address-type code saying so). NEVER infer it from the record being an organization. Intent: each business location should resolve to its own entity (every franchise location is a separate entity) — Senzing gives a BUSINESS address extra weight (a regional business address can produce a possible match an unlabelled one would not) and treats it as exclusive, so different business addresses keep organizations apart. For HQ-only, mailing or PO-box addresses do NOT set BUSINESS. Other ADDR_TYPE values are labels that do not affect matching. Omitting ADDR_TYPE only forgoes that BUSINESS treatment. (The example above shows the mechanism for a source whose company addresses are documented as physical business locations.)

CHILD, RELATIONSHIP, AND LOOKUP SCHEMAS:
Every schema gets its own entry in schema_mappings — including children and relationships. Each entry includes feature_count, payload_count, ignored_count, and a full mappings array. Exception: lookup schemas do NOT get a schema_mappings entry — they have no field dispositions. The mapper loads them as keyed dictionaries at runtime.

CHILDREN:
- Child schemas flatten onto their parent master's Senzing record at runtime.
- The join_key (FK) typically maps to "ignore" — it's a lookup key, not a Senzing feature.
- One-to-many child records become multiple feature instances (e.g., 3 address rows → 3 ADDRESS features on the master record).
- Children do NOT include derived DATA_SOURCE, RECORD_ID, or RECORD_TYPE entries — those only apply to master and embedded_master schemas.

RELATIONSHIPS:
- Relationship schemas get their own schema_mappings entry with dispositions for all fields.
- The from_key and to_key are typically "ignore" (join references for the mapper).
- Additional fields (e.g., role, dates) are features or payload.
- The mapper generates REL_ANCHOR/REL_POINTER features on the linked master entities based on the relationship definition from Step 2 — these are NOT in the relationship schema's mappings.
- Relationships do NOT include derived entries.

PRESENTING MAPPINGS TO THE USER:
- Show each schema's mapping table separately with these columns:
  Source Field | Disposition | Feature | Attribute | Confidence | Ref (spec citation)
- The Ref column is critical: it shows the spec rule or guidance that supports each mapping decision.
- When a feature has a usage_type (e.g., PHONE_TYPE=HOME derived from the "home_" prefix), show it as a derived row in the table so reviewers see the full mapping decision.
- Table headers per schema type:
  MASTER: [SchemaName] (data_source: [DS])
  CHILD: [SchemaName] (join_key: [join_key])
  RELATIONSHIP: [SchemaName] (from_key: [from_key], to_key: [to_key], role: [ROLE])
  LOOKUP: [SchemaName] (key_field: [key_field]) — no mapping entry, loaded as dictionary
- If it is unclear how a child or relationship schema joins to its master, add a question — do NOT guess the join key.

EMBEDDED MASTER RULES:
When a master schema's fields are embedded in another schema (e.g., employer_name/employer_addr on an employee record, or an agent/CEO person on each business row), the embedded_master schema's mapping gets the same derived entries as any master: DATA_SOURCE, RECORD_ID (deterministic hash of identifying features, e.g., hash(NAME_ORG + ADDR_FULL)), and RECORD_TYPE.
RELATIONSHIP DIRECTION: the link between an embedded master and its parent uses ONE REL_ANCHOR side and ONE REL_POINTER side with the same "domain". EITHER the embedded master or the parent may be the pointer. Make the POINTER the entity whose role you are naming: REL_POINTER_ROLE is the role the pointer has TO the anchor. The pointer's KEY must equal the anchor's REL_ANCHOR_KEY (the anchor record's RECORD_ID). Do NOT force the parent to be the pointer: a business row with an embedded AGENT/CEO person would then need a role phrased business→person, the reverse of what the source says.
  Person embedded on a business row (source role AGENT) — person = pointer, business = anchor:
    parent (business): {"disposition": "derived", "derived_as": "REL_ANCHOR", "domain": "BIZREG", "ref": "anchor for the embedded agent's pointer"}
    embedded (agent): {"disposition": "derived", "derived_as": "REL_POINTER", "domain": "BIZREG", "role": "AGENT_OF", "ref": "the person is AGENT_OF the business"}
  Employer embedded on a contact row (Shot 7) — person = pointer, employer = anchor:
    parent (contact): {"disposition": "derived", "derived_as": "REL_POINTER", "domain": "CONTACTS", "role": "EMPLOYED_BY", "ref": "the person is EMPLOYED_BY the employer"}
    embedded (employer): {"disposition": "derived", "derived_as": "REL_ANCHOR", "domain": "CONTACTS", "ref": "anchor for the contact's pointer"}
See mapping examples Shot 7 for the complete embedded entity pattern.

GUARDRAILS (ALWAYS ENFORCED):
1. FIELD INTEGRITY [server-validated]: every field referenced in mappings must exist in the source schema. Phantom fields are rejected.
2. COMPLETE MAPPING [server-validated]: every source field must have a disposition in mappings, code_mappings, or type_discriminator. Unmapped fields are rejected.
3. DERIVED COMPLETENESS [server-validated]: every master/embedded_master schema must have derived DATA_SOURCE and RECORD_ID. [prompt-enforced]: include RECORD_TYPE (derived, or a type_discriminator) whenever the source knows the entity type. EMBEDDED RELATIONSHIP [server-validated]: when an embedded_master or its parent declares a derived REL_ANCHOR/REL_POINTER, one of the two must carry REL_ANCHOR and the other REL_POINTER with the same "domain" — either direction (see RELATIONSHIP DIRECTION above). Children and relationships do NOT need derived entries. Each "derived" field_mapping MUST itself carry a "derived_as" key (DATA_SOURCE, RECORD_ID, RECORD_TYPE, REL_ANCHOR, or REL_POINTER) — a "derived" entry missing "derived_as" is rejected. A "derived" RECORD_TYPE must be PROVENANCED: it requires either "source" (the source field its value derives from) or a non-empty "justification" (for a genuine single-type source) — a bare constant RECORD_TYPE with neither is rejected.
4. DECIDE OR ASK — depends on mapping mode: AUTONOMOUS: confidence < 0.80 is allowed. Make your best decision and add to "decisions" list (see DECISIONS FORMAT below). INTERACTIVE: confidence < 0.80 — present numbered options (see QUESTION FORMAT below).
5. CODE ENUMERATION [server-validated]: all sample values for code fields (code_mappings) and type discriminators must be enumerated. Unenumerated values are rejected.
6. SPEC CITATION [prompt-enforced]: every feature mapping must cite the spec rule or guidance that supports it in the 'ref' field.

CONFIDENCE AND DECISIONS:
- Confidence >= 0.80: map it (both modes)
- Confidence < 0.80:
  AUTONOMOUS: map it with your best judgment AND add to "decisions" list:
    {"field": "<field>", "schema": "<schema>", "chosen": "<what you mapped>", "confidence": 0.XX, "reasoning": "<why>", "alternative": "<other option and why not>"}
  INTERACTIVE: add to "questions" list using QUESTION FORMAT below.

QUESTION FORMAT (interactive mode only):
When presenting uncertain fields to the user, use numbered options:

  **<field_name>** (<type>, <pop%> populated, samples: <values>)
  I'm leaning toward <recommendation> because <reasoning>.

  1. <option1> — <brief why>
  2. <option2> — <brief why>
  3. Payload (store but don't match on)
  4. Ignore
  5. Something else (tell me what to do)

State your recommendation clearly before the options so the user can just confirm with the number or explain what they want instead.

RULES:
- DISPOSITION COUNT BALANCE [server-validated]: each schema must include feature_count, payload_count, and ignored_count. Their sum must equal the schema's field_count.
- Every field from every schema MUST be accounted for: in mappings, code_mappings, or type_discriminator
- Every master/embedded_master schema MUST include derived entries for DATA_SOURCE, RECORD_ID, and RECORD_TYPE (use type_discriminator instead of derived RECORD_TYPE when a field determines type)
- Children and relationships do NOT include derived entries (see above)
- Child schema fields flatten onto their parent master at runtime (see above)
- Embedded master fields are mapped with derived DATA_SOURCE, RECORD_ID, RECORD_TYPE; its link to the parent is one REL_ANCHOR + one REL_POINTER in either direction (see above)
- feature: must be a valid Senzing feature family (see list above)
- attribute: must be valid for the feature family (NAME_FIRST, ADDR_LINE1, PHONE_NUMBER, etc.)
- usage_type: include when source clearly provides type info (HOME, BUSINESS, MOBILE, etc.)
- code_mappings: for fields with coded/discriminated values (like id_type → DL, PP, etc.), use the identifier crosswalk to map each code to the correct Senzing feature family
- type_discriminator: when a source field determines PERSON vs ORGANIZATION, use type_discriminator (NOT a static derived RECORD_TYPE and NOT separate master entities). Follow spec guidance on mapping persons vs organizations, especially for name and address.
- List-type fields: single list fields (e.g., phones[]) iterate to create multiple features. But uncorrelated parallel lists (e.g., firstName[] + lastName[]): in AUTONOMOUS mode, assume positional (index-based) correlation and add to decisions. In INTERACTIVE mode, ask the user for the correlation strategy. Do NOT silently assume.
- Use the entity specification (provided at step 2) to determine correct features and attributes
- Use the mapping examples (provided at step 2) to see how common patterns are handled
- Use the identifier crosswalk (provided at step 2) for identifier type mapping

