ly to the pre-built Docker images (senzing/senzingsdk-tools, senzing/init-database, etc.) — those require PostgreSQL. For Docker, use senzing/init-database to initialize the database.","IMPORTANT: Without a license, Senzing is limited to 500 Distinct Source Records (DSRs). Loading record 501 fails with SENZ9000|LIMIT. Do NOT ask about licensing if the user has 500 or fewer records — just load them. Only when the user has MORE than 500 records, ASK: 'Do you have a Senzing license? If so, paste the base64 license string and I will add it to your engine config.' Then add LICENSESTRINGBASE64 to the PIPELINE section. If they don't have a license AND records are about to be loaded on a working Senzing install, offer three options: (1) continue with just 500 of their records as a sample, (2) request a free 10-day evaluation license (250K records) right now using submit_feedback with category='license_request', or (3) request a license at https://senzing.com/request-non-prod-license/. These three options belong to that imminent load only — do NOT raise them while installing, configuring, or merely explaining Senzing, and never on a host where Senzing is not installed: there is no load there to take a sample of. Even with a license, this single-threaded LLM container environment is not recommended for more than 10,000 records — for larger datasets, recommend a dedicated loading pipeline with threaded loading (generate_scaffold topic='add_records'). License options: LICENSESTRINGBASE64 (inline base64 string in config — preferred for containers/automation) or LICENSEFILE (path to .lic file on disk). Example with license: {"PIPELINE": {"CONFIGPATH": "/etc/opt/senzing", "RESOURCEPATH": "/opt/senzing/er/resources", "SUPPORTPATH": "/opt/senzing/data", "LICENSESTRINGBASE64": "<paste-base64-here>"}, "SQL": {"CONNECTION": "sqlite3://na:na@/tmp/sqlite/G2C.db"}}"],"env_vars":{"LD_LIBRARY_PATH":"/opt/senzing/er/lib (only needed if native lib not found automatically)","PYTHONPATH":"/opt/